    "case_id": "765",
    "case_summary": "A 47-year-old woman with controllable hypertension presented with an episode of neck and back pain and loss of consciousness. She had experienced a similar episode one year prior. Her family history revealed that her uncle and two sisters had sudden cardiac death at a young age. She had taken calcium channel blocker for about one month after her first episode.\n\nOn admission, patient was unconscious with:- Body temperature: 37.5°C- Heart rate: 68 bpm- Blood pressure: 145/50 mmHg- Pupils: equal at 3.5 mm with sluggish light reflex- Absent corneal reflex- Normal lung auscultation\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Normal hematology- Mild hyperglycemia (11.92 mmol/L)- Elevated transaminases (ALT 146 U/L, AST 89 U/L)- Arterial blood gas: pH 7.213, PCO2 35.8 mmHg, PO2 396.1 mmHg, HCO3− 14.1 mmol/L, O2 saturation 99.7%, lactate 11.53 mmol/L- Initial troponin I, D-dimer, and BNP levels were normalImaging Studies:- Head and chest CT scans were unremarkable- Coronary angiography revealed normal left and right coronary arteries- Left ventricular ejection fraction: 59%ECG Findings:- First episode showed complete atrioventricular block with inferior ST-segment elevation (heart rate 45 bpm)- Second episode showed junctional escape rhythm (heart rate 32 bpm)Genetic Testing:- Whole exome sequencing performed on patient and family members showed no abnormalities",
